{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/support.fixed_time_spatial_coercivity_at_jumps","record":{"id":"support.fixed_time_spatial_coercivity_at_jumps","kind":"claim","label":"Spatial coercivity at a coefficient jump","status":"unverified","source_id":"authored_long_comparisons_20261024","locator":"Long reasoning record #fixed-time-jump-extension","statement":"For admissible gamma, 0<t<1 and c=gamma(t)>0, E[d^2-12*c*a*b^2+6*c^2*a^4]>=(c^2/1000)E[a^4], with a=Phi_xx,b=Phi_xxx,d=Phi_xxxx along its diffusion at t. Flattening gamma to c on [t-delta,t+delta) has L1 error<=2delta gamma(t+delta0), so local boundedness suffices to pass spatial moments even at jumps. This changes no retained Lean continuity hypothesis and asserts no time derivative at a jump.","assumption_ids":["scalar.paper_admissible"],"citation_ids":["authored_r8_support_fixed_time_spatial_coercivity_at_jumps"],"evidence_ids":["ordinary_deductions_review_20261026"],"boundary":"Ordinary authored deduction checked against retained mathematical inputs; no new Lean declaration, elaborated dependency, cross-model transport, Comparator acceptance or independent proof certification is asserted."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/support.fixed_time_spatial_coercivity_at_jumps","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/2f85413164f06365ea0d825befee447650da71d54080d5dd65e78a708891c560","roles":["mapped_claim"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/fixed-time-coercivity","anchor":"fixed-time-coercivity","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/fixed-time-coercivity","anchor":"fixed-time-coercivity","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n<a id=\"plateau-limit\"></a>\n\n### 4.5 A fixed-time inequality and its plateau application\n\n<a id=\"fixed-time-coercivity\"></a>\n\n**Proposition 4.1 (coercivity at a fixed time).** Let $\\gamma\\in\\mathcal U$\nand let $X$ be its associated diffusion, started at zero. Suppose that\n$0<t<1$, $\\gamma$ is continuous at $t$, and $c=\\gamma(t)>0$. With\n$a=\\Phi_{xx}$, $b=\\Phi_{xxx}$ and $d=\\Phi_{xxxx}$, evaluated at\n$(t,X_t)$, one has\n$$\n\\mathcal C_\\gamma(t):=\\mathbb E[d^2-12cab^2+6c^2a^4]\n \\ge \\frac{c^2}{1000}\\mathbb E a^4.\n\\tag{4.1}\n$$\nNeither minimality nor a constant neighbourhood of $t$ is assumed.\nThis is the common estimate behind plateau curvature and positive density.\nWhen $\\gamma$ is smooth near $t$, Itô's formula gives\n$q''=\\mathbb E b^2-2\\gamma\\mathbb E a^3$ and\n$$\n(\\mathbb E b^2)'=\\mathbb E d^2-6\\gamma\\mathbb E(ab^2),\\qquad\n(\\mathbb E a^3)'=3\\mathbb E(ab^2)-3\\gamma\\mathbb E a^4.\n$$\nDifferentiating the first identity therefore gives\n$$\nq'''(t)+2\\gamma'(t)\\mathbb E a^3=\\mathcal C_\\gamma(t).\n\\tag{4.2}\n$$\nOn a plateau the coefficient derivative vanishes. At a smooth minimizer,\n$q=t$ makes the third derivative of $q$ vanish instead.\n\n**Proof.** Fix $t<T<T'<1$ and a shrinking interval\n$[t-\\delta_n,t+\\delta_n)\\subset(0,T)$. Approximate $\\gamma$ by\n$$\nh_n=\\max\\{\\epsilon_n,\\min\\{K_n,\n                  \\eta_n\\lfloor\\gamma/\\eta_n\\rfloor\\}\\},\n$$\nwhere $0<\\epsilon_n<c<K_n$, $\\epsilon_n,\\eta_n\\downarrow0$ and\n$K_n\\uparrow\\infty$. Use $\\min(c,h_n)$ to the left of the interval,\n$c$ on it, and $\\max(c,h_n)$ to its right. Monotonicity makes these\npositive, nondecreasing, right-continuous finite-step coefficients\n$\\gamma_n$. Clipping toward $c$ outside the interval cannot increase\nthe error, and\n$$\n\\|\\gamma_n-\\gamma\\|_1\n\\le\\epsilon_n+\\eta_n+\\int_0^1(\\gamma-K_n)_+\\,ds\n +2\\delta_n\\sup_{|s-t|\\le\\delta_n}|\\gamma(s)-c|\n \\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{4.3}\n$$\nIntegrability controls the capped tail and continuity at $t$ the last\nterm. Choose smooth terminal parameters $M_n\\ge\\max(n,K_n)$.\nThe terminal values then converge uniformly to $|x|$, and the coefficient\nmasses have a common bound. The finite-step inequality of §4.4 applies\nat $t$, where every $\\gamma_n$ equals the same positive $c$.\n\nOn a fixed strip containing $t$ and ending before one, compact-strip\nregularity gives uniform spatial convergence and bounds through order\nfour. The next derivative bounds give a common spatial Lipschitz\nconstant for those derivatives. Coupling with the same Brownian motion\nand applying Gronwall gives a deterministic bound\n$\\sup_{s\\le T}|X_s^n-X_s|\\to0$. If $D_k^n=\\partial_x^k\\Phi_n$\nand $D_k=\\partial_x^k\\Phi$, then, for $1\\le k\\le4$,\n\n$$\n|D_k^n(t,X_t^n)-D_k(t,X_t)|\n\\le\\|D_k^n-D_k\\|_{\\infty,[0,T]\\times\\mathbb R}\n   +C_{k,T}|X_t^n-X_t|\\longrightarrow0.\n$$\n\nAfter the nonnegative terms are discarded, the two surviving integrands\n$z_n^2-12v_nw_n^2+6v_n^4$ and $v_n^4$ are uniformly bounded,\nwith $v_n=cD_2^n$, $w_n=cD_3^n$ and $z_n=cD_4^n$. Dominated convergence therefore passes their expectations\nto the limit. For completeness, let $V,W,Z$ bound both the limiting and approximating\n$v,w,z$, and let $e_v,e_w,e_z$ bound their coupled evaluation errors.\nUniform spatial convergence and the path bound above give these errors\nwith $e_v,e_w,e_z\\to0$. Difference-of-powers identities give\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbb E\\mathcal C(v_n,w_n,z_n)-\\mathbb E\\mathcal C(v,w,z)|\n &\\le(12W^2+24V^3)e_v+24VW e_w+2Ze_z,\\\\\n|\\mathbb E v_n^4-\\mathbb E v^4|&\\le4V^3e_v,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere $\\mathcal C(v,w,z)=z^2-12vw^2+6v^4$. These constants depend\non the common strip and coefficient mass bound, not on plateau width.\nAfter division by $c^2$, this is (4.1). Only spatial moments have been\npassed to the limit. This transfer requires neither convergence of $s_n$ or\n$j_n$ nor a uniform bound on their tail constants: those auxiliary\nquantities have already disappeared after integration.\n[OpenAI (2026a), Lemmas 2.1–2.3][fs-reg];\n[Proposition 5.1][fs-curv].\n\n<a id=\"fixed-time-jump-extension\"></a>\n\n**The spatial inequality at a coefficient jump.** Continuity at $t$ is\na sufficient hypothesis in Proposition 4.1 and in the retained sampled\nformal interface. The ordinary spatial-moment argument permits a little\nmore. Fix $c=\\gamma(t)>0$ and replace $\\gamma$ by $c$ on\n$[t-\\delta,t+\\delta)$, leaving it unchanged elsewhere. Monotonicity\npreserves both joins and right continuity. For fixed\n$0<\\delta_0<\\min(t,1-t)$ and $0<\\delta\\le\\delta_0$,\n$$\n\\|\\gamma_\\delta-\\gamma\\|_1\n\\le2\\delta\\,\\gamma(t+\\delta_0)\\longrightarrow0.\n$$\nThus local boundedness suffices for the same strip stability and coupled\nexpectation limit. Each replacement has a positive plateau at $t$, so\n(4.1) holds at a jump too, with the stated right-continuous value $c$.\nThis extension concerns spatial moments only: it supplies no time\nderivative $q'''(t)$ or $\\gamma'(t)$ at a jump and does not remove a\nhypothesis from the retained Lean declaration. Neither application below\nrequires this extension.\n\n**Corollary 4.2 (positive plateaus).** Suppose now that $\\gamma=c>0$\non an open interval $I\\subset(0,1)$ and $t\\in I$. Then (4.2) gives\n$q'''(t)=\\mathcal C_\\gamma(t)$. To establish strictness for this\nlimiting solution, choose $b\\in I$ later than $t$ and put\n$\\psi=\\Phi_\\gamma(b,\\cdot)$. This is smooth, convex, even and a\nbounded distance from $|x|$, so $\\psi'$ cannot be constant. The\nCole–Hopf formula gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{xx}(t,x)\n=\\mathbb E^{\\rm tilt}_{t,x}\\psi''\n  +c\\operatorname{Var}^{\\rm tilt}_{t,x}(\\psi'),\n$$\n$$\n\\nu_{t,x}(dy)=\n\\frac{e^{c\\psi(y)}p_{b-t}(y-x)}\n {\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{c\\psi(z)}p_{b-t}(z-x)\\,dz}\\,dy.\n$$\n\nThe tilted density is positive everywhere. Its variance of the\nnonconstant continuous function $\\psi'$ is strictly positive; its\nmean of $\\psi''$ is nonnegative. Thus $v(t,x)>0$ for every $x$, and\n$\\mathbb E v(t,X_t)^4>0$. Combining the limiting inequality with\nthe differentiated identity proves\n$q'''(t)\\ge(1000c^2)^{-1}\\mathbb E v(t,X_t)^4>0$.\nThis proves strict convexity of $q'$ on $I$.\n[OpenAI (2026a), Lemma 2.3][fs-reg]; [Proposition 5.1][fs-curv].\n\n<a id=\"zero-plateau\"></a>\n\n### 4.6 Why the zero interval remains a separate case\n\nWhen $c=0$, the heat equation gives\n$q''(t)=\\mathbb E\\Phi_{xxx}(t,X_t)^2$ without division by $c$.\nStart the drift-free evolution at an earlier point of $I$; its\nGaussian transition kernel makes the law of $X_t$ have a positive\ndensity everywhere. If the expectation were zero, continuity would\nforce $\\Phi_{xxx}(t,\\cdot)\\equiv0$. Then $\\Phi(t,\\cdot)$ would\nbe an even quadratic polynomial. Its one-Lipschitz bound forces that\npolynomial to be constant, contradicting its bounded distance from\n$|x|$. Therefore $q''(t)>0$, giving strict increase of $q'$.\n[OpenAI (2026a), Lemma 2.3][fs-reg].\n\nThe analytic certificate is thus a proved input to the gap argument in\nthis source, with the stated model and regularity assumptions. An\nabstract polynomial or flux lemma assumes those analytic hypotheses;\nthe finite-step comparisons and the transfer argument discharge them.\nInspection of the corresponding Lean sources records that construction,\nwhile compilation, scoped dependency extraction and Comparator replay\nremain distinct checks, with their observed outcomes reported in §13.\n","span":{"start_byte":62293,"end_byte":69498,"start_line":1363,"end_line":1528},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=fixed-time-coercivity","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=fixed-time-coercivity"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=support.fixed_time_spatial_coercivity_at_jumps&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=support.fixed_time_spatial_coercivity_at_jumps&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=support.fixed_time_spatial_coercivity_at_jumps&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=support.fixed_time_spatial_coercivity_at_jumps&relation=prerequisites"},"relations":[{"family":"arguments","id":"field:755fb18f7c0b2e0db67dc2980f41d155887fa4123b25fcb540e63f91c69f2b40","from":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/support.fixed_time_spatial_coercivity_at_jumps","to":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.paper_admissible","kind":"assumption_ids","semantic_class":"explicit_assumption_reference","derived":true,"owner_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/support.fixed_time_spatial_coercivity_at_jumps","source_field":"assumption_ids"},{"family":"arguments","id":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629e